# System Description: Bandwidth-Adaptive Quality of Service Management

## 1. Overview

The disclosed system comprises a data management module configured to monitor, evaluate, and dynamically respond to bandwidth constraints affecting one or more platform applications operating across one or more communication links. The system operates to preserve operational continuity and application performance by detecting bandwidth shortfalls and initiating corrective adaptation through a dedicated quality of service (QoS) interface.

## 2. System Components

### 2.1 Data Management Module

The data management module serves as the central processing entity of the system and is communicatively coupled to:

- One or more platform applications executing on a host platform (which may include, without limitation, mobile platforms, vehicular platforms, aerospace platforms, or fixed infrastructure platforms);
- One or more communication links, which may comprise wired, wireless, satellite, cellular, mesh-network, or hybrid transmission media;
- A quality of service interface configured to receive adaptation commands and translate such commands into actionable network or application-layer adjustments.

The data management module includes, or is operatively coupled to, a processor and a non-transitory computer-readable memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the module to perform the functions described herein.

### 2.2 Bandwidth Requirement Determination

The data management module is configured to determine, continuously, periodically, or upon a triggering event, a **required bandwidth** value associated with each platform application. The required bandwidth may be determined by reference to:

- Predefined application profiles or service-level parameters;
- Real-time data throughput demands generated by the application;
- Priority classifications or mission-critical designations assigned to the application;
- Historical usage patterns or predictive modeling of anticipated data load.

### 2.3 Available Bandwidth Determination

The data management module is further configured to determine a **current available bandwidth** value for each monitored communication link. This determination may be based on:

- Real-time link telemetry, including signal quality, latency, packet loss, and throughput metrics;
- Link utilization data aggregated from network management systems;
- Environmental or interference factors affecting link capacity;
- Reported or measured available capacity from network infrastructure elements.

## 3. Comparative Evaluation Function

The data management module is configured to perform a comparison between the required bandwidth of a given platform application and the current available bandwidth of the communication link(s) allocated, or available, to service that application. This comparison may be executed:

- On a continuous or periodic basis;
- Upon detection of a change in application state or link condition;
- In response to a scheduled polling interval defined by system configuration.

The comparison yields a determination as to whether the current available bandwidth is **sufficient** or **insufficient** (a "shortfall") to meet the required bandwidth of the platform application.

## 4. Shortfall Detection and Adaptation Triggering

### 4.1 Shortfall Determination

A shortfall condition is deemed to exist when the data management module determines that the current available bandwidth of one or more communication links is less than the required bandwidth of one or more platform applications, whether measured as an absolute deficit, a percentage deficit, or a deficit persisting beyond a threshold duration.

### 4.2 Adaptation Command Generation

Upon detection of a shortfall, the data management module is configured to generate and transmit an adaptation command to the quality of service interface. The adaptation command may specify one or more of the following corrective actions, without limitation:

- **Prioritization/Reprioritization:** Adjusting the relative priority of data traffic associated with the affected platform application(s) relative to other applications sharing the communication link(s);
- **Bandwidth Reallocation:** Reallocating available bandwidth among competing applications according to predefined or dynamically computed priority schemes;
- **Link Switching or Aggregation:** Directing traffic to an alternate communication link possessing sufficient available bandwidth, or aggregating multiple links to satisfy the required bandwidth;
- **Data Rate Adjustment:** Instructing the platform application to reduce its data generation rate, compression ratio, resolution, or update frequency;
- **Traffic Shaping/Throttling:** Applying rate-limiting or buffering measures to non-critical data streams to preserve bandwidth for higher-priority applications;
- **Graceful Degradation:** Selectively reducing the fidelity, frequency, or completeness of data transmitted by the affected application while maintaining core functionality.

### 4.3 Quality of Service Interface Execution

The quality of service interface, upon receipt of the adaptation command, is configured to implement the specified corrective action at the network, transport, or application layer, as appropriate, and to report execution status back to the data management module.

## 5. Feedback and Iterative Adaptation

The data management module is further configured to monitor the effect of the implemented adaptation by re-evaluating the required bandwidth and current available bandwidth following execution of the adaptation command. Where the shortfall persists, the data management module may generate a subsequent, escalated, or alternative adaptation command. This feedback loop may continue iteratively until the shortfall is resolved or until a maximum adaptation threshold or fallback condition is reached.

## 6. Optional Features

The system may further include:

- A logging subsystem configured to record shortfall events, adaptation commands, and outcomes for diagnostic, regulatory, or audit purposes;
- A configurable policy engine enabling operator-defined rules governing prioritization among multiple platform applications during concurrent shortfall conditions;
- A predictive analytics module configured to forecast prospective shortfalls based on trend analysis, enabling preemptive adaptation prior to actual shortfall occurrence;
- A user interface or reporting mechanism configured to present shortfall events and adaptation actions to a human operator, with optional manual override capability.

## 7. Illustrative Claim Language (Sample)

> A system comprising: a data management module configured to determine a required bandwidth of at least one platform application; determine a current available bandwidth of at least one communication link; compare the required bandwidth to the current available bandwidth; and, upon determining that the current available bandwidth is insufficient to satisfy the required bandwidth, transmit an adaptation command to a quality of service interface, wherein the quality of service interface is configured to adjust at least one operational parameter of the at least one platform application or the at least one communication link in response to the adaptation command.

